@vonsteinen Could be double topping for now, look at JP 10 yr chart, right back to where we were in March. But even if we break through in any erratic way, I think BoJ comes in. If it breaks through in an orderly way higher, I think the market can handle it without it causing some type of collapse. 

I'll be the first to admit that some really funky action or spikes in yields will cause equities to pullback, and we could get a scare in coming months that equates to a 5 or 10% pullback, but I don't think it stops the longterm trajectory here of equities higher. In other words, 6 months or a year from now we could be pushing past 5% on US 10 yr and don't think it crashes things, if we spike there in 3 weeks, then we'll have issues, but I don't think that will happen. 

The market will need to acclimate, both here and around the world, and there will be jitters, but ultimately, I think this fear that if our 10 yr breaks above 4.75% then equity rally is off unless it immediately pulls back is incorrect. Eventually, the market will learn not to sell off every time yields go up. I mean we're getting a nice, more normal, steepening yield curve here. It can steepen further with equities continuing to march higher.

@EdKrassen There are so many things wrong about that statement from a factual and strategic perspective, I don't know what to say. In process of reading a book called Chokepoints: American Power in the Age of Economic Warfare by Edward Fishman. He's a dem, worked for Obama Treasury dept. or something, on some energy councils, now teaches at Colombia, in other words, a liberal and not in agreement with me on a lot of things, but in the book he details the rise of economic warfare since 9/11. 

This increase in sanctions, tariffs, etc. is not new and not partisan. It has increasingly become part of the US foreign policy toolkit over the last 24 years, and the main goal is for the US to be able to achieve its geopolitical aims without having to kill people. I know its bad for margins at the railroad companies Buffett owns, but he should realize that the US has not been "free trade" for 250 years. And if less innocent people around the world have to die and less US vets have to come home in body bags, then maybe some tariffs aren't so bad. 

Alexander Hamilton put in multiple tariff laws as part of Washington's admin including different tariff Acts of 1789, 1790, and 1792. We are currently engaged in an economic war with China that hopefully doesn't have to become kinetic. Whoever is president next will find it very hard to remove all the tariffs that Trump eventually settles on, even if its a Dem. To me this is more deep state, geopolitical stuff than partisan politics in many ways. Now can one argue about the style of how this has been rolled out? Certainly. But there's deeper, more important strategic considerations at play here than just some populist rhetoric.

@dampedspring Exactly. I was posting about this coming a few weeks ago when the latest Monthly Treasury Statement was released. Back in Feb & Mar US gov't borrowed only $4.59 billion new net from the public thanks to extraordinary measures/debt ceiling. Note last line from MTS (Table 7) below-only $2B in Mar vs $262B in Oct. 

FY to date, only $607B in total borrowing from the public compared to $1.162 Trillion a year ago. Again, this is all thanks to debt ceiling, and what it means is that once debt ceiling is lifted, there's about $600 or $700 billion in new debt that will need to be floated to just make up for what we're not issuing now, on top of continuing needs. 

For further evidence, note Table 2, Reduction of Operating Cash: FY to date: $480 Billion whereas last year there was actually $118 Billion increase in the same period.

