@infraa_ From Google AI, asked What was average home cost in San Francisco in 1971? 
The median price of a home in San Francisco in 1971 was $16,200. When adjusted for inflation, that's equivalent to $182,145 in 2023 dollars.
In 1970, the median household income in San Francisco was about $10,500, so it took less than three years of annual income to buy a home. However, the value-to-income ratio has changed significantly since then. In 2022, the average household income in San Francisco was about $137,000, which was only about 10% of the value of a typical home

@AlecStapp Maybe it’s time for American Equity: Shares in the USA. One major issue with high-skilled immigration is that those who are from here and whose ancestors built this country have no explicit recognition or benefit from this fact. A newly-naturalized citizen has the exact same rights and privileges as someone whose family built this country, whether white, black, Asian or otherwise. It’s not about race, it’s about equity in the USA.
Why not begin giving citizens one share in the USA for every year they pay taxes, regardless of $1 or $1 billion in taxes paid. Over time, these shares will grow and can be passed down to heirs. Benefits accruing from said shares could be similar to those veterans receive. You have 10 shares, you get a lifetime pass to National Parks. 25 shares, a better mortgage rate. 50 shares, GI Bill-like tuition for your kids. 75 shares, free health insurance, etc.
Allow Americans to build generational wealth by being tax-paying, productive citizens. Times have changed. We’re no longer a wide open, frontier nation with no social safety net. Allowing everyone, no matter how recent they arrived here, to participate equally with those who, along with their ancestors, built this country just doesn’t seem fair anymore. How would you feel if you worked at a startup for 10 years and then a new hire joined right before the IPO and was granted the same number of options you earned over a decade? AmeriShares could not only recognize long-term service to the country but also add a sense of fairness for that 50-year-old factory worker who sees a “high-skilled” immigrant driving down his street in a new Tesla.

